window.VS_DAILY=[["It can ruin your life only if it ruins your character. Otherwise it cannot harm you - inside or out.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.8",["Virtue & Courage","Mind & Self"]],["That sort of person is bound to do that. You might as well resent a fig tree for secreting juice. (Anyway, before very long you'll both be dead - dead and soon forgotten.)","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.6",["Anger","Mind & Self"]],["To stand up straight - not straightened.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 3.5",["Virtue & Courage"]],["The best revenge is to be unlike him who performed the injury.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.6",["Anger","Virtue & Courage"]],["To stop talking about what the good man is like, and just be one.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 10.16",["Virtue & Courage"]],["The world is nothing but change. Our life is only perception.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.3 (quoting Democritus)",["Life","Mind & Self"]],["Frightened of change? But what can exist without it? What's closer to nature's heart?","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.18",["Fear & Suffering","Life"]],["Choose not to be harmed - and you won't feel harmed. Don't feel harmed - and you haven't been.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.7",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["Not to display anger or other emotions. To be free of passion and yet full of love.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 1.9",["Anger","Mind & Self"]],["So other people hurt me? That's their problem. Their character and actions are not mine. What is done to me is ordained by nature, what I do by my own.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.25",["Mind & Self","Anger"]],["Practice really hearing what people say. Do your best to get inside their minds.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.53",["Friendship"]],["People exist for one another. You can instruct or endure them.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.59",["Friendship"]],["Self-reliance, always. And Cheerfulness.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 1.16",["Mind & Self","Happiness"]],["How to act: Cheerfulness. Without requiring other people's help. Or serenity supplied by others.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 3.5",["Happiness","Mind & Self"]],["Look inward. Don't let the true nature or value of anything elude you.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.3",["Mind & Self"]],["You cannot lose another life than the one you're living now, or live another one than the one you're losing.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.14",["Time","Death"]],["Look at the past - empire after empire - and from that, extrapolate the future: the same thing. No escape from the rhythm of events.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.49",["Life","Time"]],["To read attentively - not to be satisfied with 'just getting the gist of it'.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 1.7",["Mind & Self"]],["The sense he gave of staying on the path rather than being kept on it.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 1.15",["Virtue & Courage"]],["Today I escaped from anxiety. Or no, I discarded it, because it was within me, in my own perceptions - not outside.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 9.13",["Fear & Suffering","Mind & Self"]],["Don't waste the rest of your time here worrying about other people - unless it affects the common good. It will keep you from doing anything useful.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 3.4",["Time","Mind & Self"]],["The things ordained for you - teach yourself to be at one with those. And the people who share them with you - treat them with love. With real love.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.39",["Life","Friendship"]],["To love only what happens, what was destined. No greater Harmony.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.57",["Life","Happiness"]],["Characteristics of the rational soul: Self-perception, self-examination, and the power to make of itself whatever it wants.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 11.1",["Mind & Self"]],["To put up with discomforts and not make demands.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 1.5",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["My only fear is doing something contrary to human nature - the wrong thing - the wrong way - or at the wrong time.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.20",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["That kindness is invincible, provided it's sincere - not ironic or an act.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 11.18",["Virtue & Courage","Friendship"]],["If they've injured you, then they're the ones who suffer for it. But have they?","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 9.38",["Anger","Mind & Self"]],["Enter their minds, and you'll find the judges you're so afraid of - and how judiciously they judge themselves.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 9.18",["Fear & Suffering","Mind & Self"]],["The mind is that which is roused and directed by itself. It makes of itself what it chooses. It makes what it chooses of its own experience.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.8",["Mind & Self"]],["Think of yourself as dead. You have lived your life. Now take what's left and live it properly.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.56",["Death","Life"]],["Death. The end of sense-perception, of being controlled by our emotions, of mental activity, of enslavement to our bodies.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.28",["Death"]],["There is nothing bad in undergoing change - or good in emerging from it.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.42",["Life","Fear & Suffering"]],["'A little wisp of a soul carrying a corpse.' - Epictetus","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.41",["Death","Mind & Self"]],["Before long, all existing things will be transformed, to rise like smoke (assuming all things become one), or be dispersed in fragments.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.4",["Life","Death"]],["The world is maintained by change - in the elements and in the things they compose. That should be enough for you; treat it as an axiom.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.3",["Life"]],["An ordered world or a mishmash. But still an order. Can there be order within you and not in everything else? In things so different, so dispersed, so intertwined?","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.43",["Life","Mind & Self"]],["Not to assume it's impossible because you find it hard. But to recognize that if it's humanly possible, you can do it too.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.19",["Virtue & Courage"]],["If you don't have a consistent goal in life, you can't live it in a consistent way.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 11.21",["Life"]],["Alexander the Great and his mule driver both died and the same thing happened to both. They were absorbed alike into the life force of the world, or dissolved alike into atoms.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.24",["Death"]],["Or is it your reputation that's bothering you? But look at how soon we're all forgotten. The abyss of endless time that swallows it all.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.3",["Death","Time"]],["Joy for humans lies in human actions. Human actions: Kindness to others, contempt for the senses, the interrogation of appearances, observation of nature and events in nature.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.26",["Happiness","Friendship"]],["Anywhere you can lead your life, you can lead a good one.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.16",["Life","Virtue & Courage"]],["The things you think about determine the quality of your mind. Your soul takes on the color of your thoughts.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.16",["Mind & Self"]],["You could leave life right now. Let that determine what you do and say and think.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.11",["Death","Life"]],["Forget everything else. Keep hold of this alone and remember it: each of us lives only now, this brief instant. The rest has been lived already, or is impossible to see.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 3.10",["Time","Life"]],["When you wake up in the morning, tell yourself: the people I deal with today will be meddling, ungrateful, arrogant, dishonest, jealous, and surly.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.1",["Anger","Friendship"]],["External things are not the problem. It's your assessment of them. Which you can erase right now.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.47",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["That every event is the right one. Look closely and you'll see. Not just the right one overall, but right.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.10",["Life","Mind & Self"]],["It's quite possible to be a good man without anyone realizing it.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.67",["Virtue & Courage"]],["The impediment to action advances action. What stands in the way becomes the way.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.20",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["At dawn, when you have trouble getting out of bed, tell yourself: 'I have to go to work - as a human being.'","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.1",["Virtue & Courage","Life"]],["You have to assemble your life yourself - action by action. And be satisfied if each one achieves its goal, as far as it can. No one can keep that from happening.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.32",["Life","Happiness"]],["If it's not right, don't do it. If it's not true, don't say it.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 12.17",["Virtue & Courage"]],["Just that you do the right thing. The rest doesn't matter.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.2",["Virtue & Courage"]],["Learn to ask of all action, 'Why are they doing that?' Starting with your own.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 10.37",["Mind & Self"]],["Not to live as if you had endless years ahead of you. Death overshadows you. While you're alive and able - be good.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.17",["Death","Time"]],["To pass through this brief life as nature demands. To give it up without complaint.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.48",["Death","Life"]],["Don't look down on death, but welcome it. It too is one of the things required by nature.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 9.3",["Death"]],["If there were anything harmful on the other side of death, they would have made sure that the ability to avoid it was within you.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.11",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["Close to forgetting it all, close to being forgotten.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.21",["Death","Time"]],["Soon you'll be ashes, or bones. A mere name, at most - and even that is just a sound, an echo. The things we want in life are empty, stale, and trivial.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.33",["Death","Wealth"]],["All that you see will soon have vanished, and those who see it vanish will vanish themselves, and the ones who reached old age have no advantage over the untimely dead.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 9.33",["Death","Time"]],["Time is a river, a violent current of events, glimpsed once and already carried past us, and another follows and is gone.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.43",["Time","Life"]],["Everything transitory - the knower and the known.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.35",["Life","Death"]],["Death and life, success and failure, pain and pleasure, wealth and poverty, all these happen to good and bad alike, and they are neither noble nor shameful - and hence neither good or bad.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.11",["Life","Death"]],["You should meditate often on the connection of all things in the universe and their relationship to each other.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.38",["Life"]],["Nowhere you can go is more peaceful - more free of interruptions - than your own soul.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.3",["Mind & Self","Happiness"]],["The mind is the ruler of the soul. It should remain unstirred by agitations of the flesh - gentle and violent ones alike.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.26",["Mind & Self"]],["You can discard most of the junk that clutters your mind - things that exist only there - and clear out space for yourself.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 9.32",["Mind & Self"]],["What am I doing with my soul? Interrogate yourself, to find out what inhabits your so-called mind and what kind of soul you have now.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.11",["Mind & Self"]],["Your ability to control your thoughts - treat it with respect. It's all that protects your mind from false perceptions.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 3.9",["Mind & Self"]],["At this moment, I have what common Nature wants me to have in this moment, and I'm doing what my own nature wants me to be doing at this moment.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.25",["Life","Time"]],["Do not give the circumstances the power to arouse anger or grief. Instead, control yourself deliberately, and let your desire direct itself only to what is within your power.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.47",["Anger","Grief"]],["What I do? I attribute it to human beneficence. What is done to me? I accept it - and attribute it to the gods, and that source from which all things together flow.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.23",["Life","Giving"]],["Stop allowing your mind to be a slave, to be jerked about by selfish impulses, to kick against fate and the present, and to mistrust the future.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.2",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["In applying one's whole soul to doing right and speaking the truth. There remains only the enjoyment of living a linked succession of good deeds, with not the slightest gap between them.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 12.29",["Virtue & Courage","Happiness"]],["If you've seen the present then you've seen everything - as it's been since the beginning, as it will be forever. The same substance, the same form.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.37",["Time","Life"]],["Continual awareness of all time and space, of the size and life span of the things around us. A grape seed in infinite space. A half twist of a corkscrew against eternity.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 10.17",["Time","Life"]],["The world as a living being - one nature, one soul. Keep that in mind. And how everything feeds into that single experience, moves with a single motion.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.40",["Life"]],["My city and state are Rome as Antoninus. But as a human being? The world.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.44",["Life","Friendship"]],["Forget the future. When and if it comes, you'll have the same resources to draw on - the same logos.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.8",["Fear & Suffering","Time"]],["You don't have to turn this into something. It doesn't have to upset you. Things can't shape your decisions by themselves.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.52",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["Stop whatever you're doing for a moment and ask yourself: am I afraid of death because I won't be able to do this anymore?","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 10.29",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["You need to avoid certain things in your train of thought: everything random, everything irrelevant. And certainly everything self-important or malicious.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 3.4",["Mind & Self"]],["Don't let your imagination be crushed by life as a whole... Stick with the situation at hand.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.36",["Fear & Suffering","Mind & Self"]],["What injures the hive injures the bee.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.54",["Friendship"]],["To be like the rock that the waves keep crashing over. It stands unmoved and the raging of the sea falls still around it.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.49",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["The thing itself was no misfortune at all; to endure it and prevail is great good fortune.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.49",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["Don't pay attention to other people's minds. Look straight ahead, where nature is leading you - nature in general, through the things that happen to you; and your own nature, through your own actions.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 7.55",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["Some things are rushing into existence, others out of it. Some of what now exists is already gone. Change and flux constantly remake the world, just as the incessant progression of time remakes eternity.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 6.15",["Life","Time"]],["Keep in mind how fast things pass by and are gone - those that are now, and those to come. Existence flows past us like a river: the 'what' is in constant flux, the 'why' has a thousand variations.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 5.23",["Time","Life"]],["Possibilities: i. To keep on living (you should be used to it by now) ii. To end it (it was your choice, after all) iii. To die (having met your obligations) Those are the only options. Reason for optimism.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 10.22",["Death","Life"]],["Remember: you shouldn't be surprised that a fig tree produces figs, not the world what it produces. A good doctor isn't surprised when his patients have fevers, or a helmsman when the wind blows against him.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 8.15",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["What dying is - and that if you look at it in the abstract and break down your imaginary ideas of it by logical analysis, you realize that it's nothing but a process of nature, which only children can be afraid of.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 2.12",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["People who are excited by posthumous fame forget that the people who remember them will soon die too... until their memory, passed from one to another like a candle flame, gutters and goes out.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.19",["Death"]],["People try to get away from it all - to the country, to the beach, to the mountains. You always wish that you could too. Which is idiotic: you can get away from it anytime you like. By going within.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.3",["Mind & Self","Happiness"]],["'If you seek tranquility, do less.' Or (more accurately) do what's essential - what the logos of a social being requires, and in the requisite way. Which brings a double satisfaction: to do less, better.","Marcus Aurelius","Meditations, Book 4.24",["Happiness","Mind & Self"]],["Let us give benefits in the way in which we would want to receive them. Above all, that means doing so willingly, quickly, and with no hesitation.","Seneca","On Benefits 2.1.1 to 2.1.2",["Giving"]],["Receiving a benefit with gratitude is the first installment of its repayment.","Seneca","On Benefits 2.22.1",["Giving"]],["The law that governs benefits between two people is this: one of them should immediately forget that the benefit was given; the other should never forget that it was received.","Seneca","On Benefits 2.10.4",["Giving"]],["We never wait for absolute certainty, since discovery of the truth is arduous, but we follow what is likely to be true. This is the path we follow in accomplishing all our tasks.","Seneca","On Benefits 4.33.2",["Mind & Self"]],["The bookkeeping for benefits is quite simple. A certain amount is disbursed; if there is any repayment at all, then it is a profit. If there is no repayment, it is not a loss. I gave it only in order to give.","Seneca","On Benefits 1.2.3",["Giving"]],["It does not matter what you bear, but how you bear it.","Seneca","On Providence, 2",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["Sometimes even to live is an act of courage.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 78",["Life","Virtue & Courage"]],["It is not that we have a brief length of time to live, but that we squander a great deal of that time.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 1",["Time","Life"]],["Life, it is thanks to death that you are precious in my eyes.","Seneca","Consolation to Marcia, 20",["Death","Life"]],["Life is long, if only you knew how to use it.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 2",["Life","Time"]],["You are unfortunate in my judgement, for you have never been unfortunate. You have passed through life with no antagonist; no one will know what you were capable of, not even yourself.","Seneca","On Providence, 4",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["How nice it is to have out-worn one's desires and left them behind.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XII",["Happiness","Mind & Self"]],["Greed is satisfied by nothing, but nature finds satisfaction even in scant measures.","Seneca","Consolation to Helvia, 10",["Wealth","Happiness"]],["What is the need to weep at parts of life? All life is worthy of our tears: Fresh problems will press upon you before you have done with the old ones.","Seneca","Consolation to Marcia, 11",["Grief","Life"]],["How can you wonder your travels do you no good, when you carry yourself around with you? You are saddled with the very thing that drove you away.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XXVIII",["Mind & Self"]],["If you really want to escape the things that harass you, what you're needing is not to be in a different place but to be a different person.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter CIV",["Mind & Self"]],["You are running away in your own company.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XXVIII",["Mind & Self"]],["Away with the world's opinion of you - it's always unsettled and divided.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XXVI",["Mind & Self"]],["For the wise man regards wealth as a slave, the fool as a master.","Seneca","On the Happy Life, 26",["Wealth","Virtue & Courage"]],["If you shape your life according to nature, you will never be poor; if according to people's opinions, you will never be rich.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XVI",["Wealth","Happiness"]],["Where money is concerned, the ideal amount is one that does not fall into poverty and yet is not far removed from poverty.","Seneca","On the Tranquillity of the Mind, 8",["Wealth"]],["Besides, a man who follows someone else not only does not find anything, he is not even looking.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XXXIII",["Mind & Self"]],["You ask what is the proper limit to a person's wealth? First, having what is essential, and second, having what is enough.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter II",["Wealth"]],["When one has lost a friend one's eyes should be neither dry nor streaming. Tears, yes, there should be, but no lamentation.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter LXIII",["Grief","Friendship"]],["There are more things, Lucilius, likely to frighten us than there are to crush us; we suffer more often in imagination than in reality.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 13.4",["Fear & Suffering"]],["At whatever point you leave life, if you leave it in the right way, it is a whole.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter LXXVII",["Death","Life"]],["No one is so ignorant as not to know that some day he must die.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter LXXVII",["Death","Life"]],["Remaining dry and sober takes a good deal more strength of will when everyone about one is puking drunk.","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XVIII",["Virtue & Courage"]],["We should live with the conviction: 'I wasn't born for one particular corner: The whole world's my home country.'","Seneca","Letters from a Stoic, Letter XXVIII",["Life","Mind & Self"]],["While we are postponing, life speeds by.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, 1.2",["Time","Life"]],["No one will bring back the years; no one will restore you to yourself.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 8",["Time","Life"]],["You fear everything as mortals but desire to have everything as gods.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 3",["Fear & Suffering","Death"]],["The greatest obstacle to living is expectation, which depends on tomorrow and wastes today.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 9",["Time","Life"]],["Present time is very short, so short, indeed, that some people think there is none.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 10",["Time"]],["You live as though you were going to live for ever, at no time taking thought for your weakness.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 4",["Death","Life"]],["Begin at once to live, and count each separate day as a separate life.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, 17.10",["Life","Time"]],["Men are tight-fisted in keeping control of their fortunes, but when it comes to the matter of wasting time, they are positively extravagant in the one area where there is honour in being miserly.","Seneca","On the Shortness of Life, 3",["Time","Wealth"]],["Greatness of spirit, as I understand it, is unshaken, solid through and through, balanced and stable from its base on up, the sort of thing that bad natures cannot contain.","Seneca","On Anger, Book 2, Section 20",["Virtue & Courage","Anger"]],["Anger has this particular evil trait: it is unwilling to be controlled.","Seneca","On Anger, Book 1, Section 19",["Anger"]],["The best course is to reject straightaway the initial prickings of anger, to fight against its first sparks, and to struggle not to succumb to it.","Seneca","On Anger, Book 1, Section 8",["Anger"]],["To avoid being angry with individuals, you must pardon the whole mass; you must grant forgiveness to the entire human race.","Seneca","On Anger, Book 2, Section 10",["Anger","Virtue & Courage"]],["If we want to be fair judges in all matters, let us convince ourselves that none of us is without fault.","Seneca","On Anger, Book 2, Section 28",["Anger","Virtue & Courage"]],["It is the mark of a great spirit to regard wrongs as beneath contempt.","Seneca","On Anger, Book 2, Section 32",["Anger","Virtue & Courage"]],["How much better it is to heal an injury than to avenge it!","Seneca","On Anger, Book 3, Section 27",["Anger"]],["If a man knows not to which port he sails, no wind is favorable.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 71.3",["Life","Mind & Self"]],["True happiness, therefore, resides in virtue.","Seneca","On the Happy Life, Chapter 16",["Happiness","Virtue & Courage"]],["Let us so order our minds as if we had come to the very end. Let us postpone nothing.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 101.7",["Death","Time"]],["We do not fear death; we fear the thought of death. For death itself is always the same distance from us.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 30.17",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["He who has learned to die has unlearned slavery.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 26.10",["Death","Virtue & Courage"]],["What is death? It is either the end, or a process of change. I have no fear of ceasing to exist; it is the same as not having begun.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 65.24",["Death"]],["He who does not wish to die cannot have wished to live. For life is granted to us with the reservation that we shall die; to this end our path leads.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 30.10",["Death","Life"]],["We do not suddenly fall on death, but advance towards it by slight degrees; we die every day.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 24.20",["Death","Time"]],["You will die, not because you are ill, but because you are alive.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 78.6",["Death","Life"]],["The happy life is one that is in harmony with its own nature.","Seneca","On the Happy Life, 3",["Happiness","Life"]],["When friendship is settled, you must trust; before friendship is formed, you must pass judgment.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 3.2",["Friendship"]],["Regard him as loyal, and you will make him loyal.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 3.3",["Friendship"]],["Ponder for a long time whether you shall admit a given person to your friendship; but when you have decided to admit him, welcome him with all your heart and soul.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 3.2",["Friendship"]],["Associate with those who will make a better man of you. Welcome those whom you yourself can improve.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 7.8",["Friendship","Mind & Self"]],["He who regards himself only, and enters upon friendships for this reason, reckons wrongly.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 9.8",["Friendship"]],["The wise man is self-sufficient, that he can do without friends, not that he desires to do without them.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 9.3",["Friendship","Mind & Self"]],["A friend should be retained in the spirit; such a friend can never be absent.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 55.11",["Friendship"]],["You must live for your neighbour, if you would live for yourself.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 48.2",["Friendship","Giving"]],["No good thing is pleasant to possess, without friends to share it.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 6.4",["Friendship","Happiness"]],["The power of the mind resides in being unconquerable, experienced in life, calm in action, and possessed of much kindness and concern for those with whom it has dealings.","Seneca","On the Happy Life, 4",["Virtue & Courage","Mind & Self"]],["If an evil has been pondered beforehand, the blow is gentle when it comes.","Seneca","Moral Letters to Lucilius, 76.34",["Fear & Suffering","Mind & Self"]],["The soul, imprisoned as it has been in this gloomy and darkened house, seeks the open sky whenever it can, and in the contemplation of the universe finds rest.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 65.17",["Mind & Self"]],["He is a great man who uses earthenware dishes as if they were silver; but he is equally great who uses silver as if it were earthenware.","Seneca","Moral Letters to Lucilius, Letter 5.6",["Wealth","Virtue & Courage"]],["The wise person's purpose in life is not to accomplish at all hazards what they attempt, but to do everything rightly.","Seneca","Moral Letters to Lucilius, 85.32",["Virtue & Courage"]],["We cannot forbid these feelings any more than we can summon them.","Seneca","Moral Letters to Lucilius, Letter 11.6",["Mind & Self"]],["Above all, my dear Lucilius, make this your business: learn how to feel joy.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, Letter 23.3",["Happiness"]],["He should continue to remember, but should cease to mourn.","Seneca","Letters to Lucilius, 99.24",["Grief"]],["Kindly remember that he whom you call your slave sprang from the same stock, is smiled upon by the same skies, and on equal terms with yourself breathes, lives, and dies.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 47.10",["Friendship","Virtue & Courage"]],["Treat your inferiors as you would be treated by your betters. And as often as you reflect how much power you have over a slave, remember that your master has just as much power over you.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 47.11",["Virtue & Courage","Friendship"]],["I propose to value them according to their character, and not according to their duties. Each man acquires his character for himself, but accident assigns his duties.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 47.15",["Virtue & Courage"]],["Show me a man who is not a slave; one is a slave to lust, another to greed, another to ambition, and all men are slaves to fear.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 47.17",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["No servitude is more disgraceful than that which is self-imposed.","Seneca","Moral Letters, 47.17",["Mind & Self"]],["For we must employ Nature as our guide; it is she whom reason looks to, she whose counsel it takes. It is therefore one and the same thing to live happily and to live in accordance with nature.","Seneca","On the Happy Life, 8",["Happiness","Life"]],["Conduct will not be right unless the will to act is right; for this is the source of conduct. Nor, again, can the will be right without a right attitude of mind; for this is the source of the will.","Seneca","Moral Letters to Lucilius, Letter 95.57",["Virtue & Courage","Mind & Self"]],["Set aside a certain number of days, during which you shall be content with the scantiest and cheapest fare, with coarse and rough dress, saying to yourself the while: 'Is this the condition that I feared?'","Seneca","Moral Letters, Letter 18.5",["Wealth","Fear & Suffering"]],["If you grieve for your son's death, it is an accusation of the time when he was born; for at birth his death was proclaimed; into this condition he was fathered, this was the fate that accompanied him immediately from the womb.","Seneca","Consolation to Marcia, 10",["Grief","Death"]],["What harm is there while you are kissing your child to say softly, 'Tomorrow you will die'.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 3, Chapter 24.87",["Death","Grief"]],["Enable my mind to adapt itself to whatever comes to pass.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 2.21",["Mind & Self"]],["First say to yourself, what manner of man you want to be; when you have settled this, act upon it in all you do.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 3, Chapter 23.1",["Virtue & Courage","Life"]],["Difficulties are the things that show what men are.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.1",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["You should drop your desire; do not covet many things, and you will get what you want.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 3, Chapter 9.22",["Happiness","Wealth"]],["If you take on a role that is beyond your powers, you not only disgrace yourself in that role, but you neglect the role that you were capable of fulfilling.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 37",["Mind & Self","Virtue & Courage"]],["Examine who you are... For you are capable of understanding the divine governance of the universe, and of reasoning on what follows from that.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 10",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["When you are about to undertake some action, remind yourself what sort of action it is.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 4",["Mind & Self"]],["But it is a much finer thing to be happy, to have a peaceful and undisturbed mind, to have what concerns you dependent on nobody but yourself.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 4, Chapter 4.36",["Happiness","Mind & Self"]],["Philosophers exhort us not to be contented with mere learning, but to add practice also, and then training.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 9.13",["Virtue & Courage"]],["Besides: do you think that I fall into evil voluntarily, and miss the good? Heaven forbid. What, then, is the cause of my going wrong? Ignorance.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 26",["Mind & Self","Virtue & Courage"]],["Only consider at what price you sell your own will and choice, man: if for nothing else, that you may not sell it cheap.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 2.33",["Virtue & Courage","Mind & Self"]],["It starts with knowing yourself, and what value you set upon yourself.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 2.11",["Mind & Self"]],["You will do your part, and I mine: It is yours to kill, and mine to die without trembling.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 2.19",["Death","Virtue & Courage"]],["To a rational creature, only what is contrary to reason is unendurable: but everything rational he can endure.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 2.1",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["To make the best of what is in our power, and take the rest as it naturally happens.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 1.17",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["Why do you not study to be contented with what is allotted to you?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 1.27",["Happiness"]],["What does virtue achieve? Peace of Mind.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 4.5",["Virtue & Courage","Happiness"]],["Now if virtue promises happiness, an untroubled mind and serenity, then progress towards virtue is certainly progress towards each of these.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 4.3",["Virtue & Courage","Happiness"]],["Do not ask things to happen as you wish, but wish them to happen as they do happen, and your life will go smoothly.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 8",["Life","Happiness"]],["Most of us fear the deadening of the body... but the deadening of the soul concerns us not a bit.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 5.4",["Mind & Self"]],["Have you not received faculties which give you the power to endure everything that happens?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 6.28",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["Bring on me now, O Zeus, whatever difficulty you will, for I have the means and the resources granted to me by yourself to bring honour to myself through whatever may come to pass.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 6.37",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["Yet I undertake to show you that you have the equipment and resources for greatness of soul and a courageous spirit: you show me what occasion you have for complaint and reproach!","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 6.43",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["For what is required in reasoning? To establish what is true, to reject what is false, and to suspend judgement in doubtful cases.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 7.5",["Mind & Self"]],["When one is asked where one is from, never to say 'I am an Athenian', or 'I am a Corinthian', but rather 'I am a citizen of the universe'.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 9.1",["Life"]],["It is necessary for you to become a student, that creature which every one laughs at, if you really desire to make an examination of your judgments.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 11.39",["Mind & Self"]],["But this, as you are quite aware, is not the work of a single hour or day.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 11.40",["Time","Virtue & Courage"]],["For he is free for whom all things happen in accordance with his choice, and whom no one can restrain.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.9",["Mind & Self"]],["True instruction is this: learning to will that things happen as they do. And how do they happen? As the appointer of them has appointed.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.15",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["Socrates was not in prison, for he was willingly there.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.23",["Mind & Self","Virtue & Courage"]],["Do you not know how very small a part you are compared to the whole? That is, as to the body, for as to reason you are neither worse nor less, than the gods.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.26",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["For greatness of reason is not measured by length or height, but by its judgment.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.26",["Mind & Self"]],["For what, then, have you been made accountable? For that which alone is in your power, the proper use of your impressions.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.34",["Mind & Self"]],["Then not to be angry, or lose your temper, is that not acceptable to the gods?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 13.2",["Anger"]],["Will you not bear with your own brother, who has Zeus as his forebear and is born as a son of the same seed as you, and is of the same high descent?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 13.3",["Friendship","Anger"]],["He (Zeus) has assigned to each man a director, his own personal daemon.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 14.12",["Mind & Self"]],["Never to disobey, never to accuse, never to find fault with anything that god has bestowed, never to do or suffer unwillingly and with a bad grace that is inevitable.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 14.16",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["In every circumstance I will preserve the governing part in accord with nature.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 15.4",["Mind & Self"]],["Bring him to me, and I will tell him; but to you I have nothing to say about his anger.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 15.5",["Anger"]],["In short, then, remember this, that if you attach value to anything outside the sphere of choice, you destroy that choice.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 4, Chapter 4.23",["Mind & Self"]],["It was fitting, then, that the gods have placed this alone in our own power, the most excellent faculty of all which rules all the others, the power to deal rightly with our impressions.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 1.7",["Mind & Self"]],["But what, then, is the admirable thing? To understand the will of nature.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 17.13",["Mind & Self","Life"]],["Some things are up to us, and others are not.","Epictetus","Enchiridion 1",["Mind & Self"]],["Choose, then, whether you want to be a drunkard and pleasing to those people, or sober and unpleasing to them.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 4, Chapter 2.7",["Virtue & Courage","Friendship"]],["Remembering that it is impossible to rub up against a person who is covered in soot without getting some of the soot on oneself.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 3, Chapter 16.3",["Friendship","Mind & Self"]],["It is not the things themselves that disturb people but their judgements about those things.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 5",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["Show them their error, and you will see how they will amend their faults; but, if they do not see it, they have nothing higher than their personal opinion to rely on.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 18.4",["Anger"]],["How have you suddenly become so wise that you are angry at others as though they were mere fools?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 18.11",["Anger"]],["If you give up on these things, and look upon them as nothing, with whom will you still be angry? But as long as you admire them, be angry with yourself rather than with others.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 18.12",["Anger","Wealth"]],["I do not say you may not groan, but do not groan within yourself.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 18.19",["Fear & Suffering"]],["Who then is the invincible man? He whom nothing outside of the sphere of choice can disconcert.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 18.21",["Virtue & Courage","Mind & Self"]],["He is a wise man who does not grieve for the things which he has not, but rejoices for those which he has.","Epictetus","Fragments, CXXIX",["Happiness","Grief"]],["When a person possesses some superiority, or at least thinks he does when in fact he does not, if he be uneducated, he will necessarily be puffed up because of it.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 19.1",["Mind & Self"]],["It is a man's own judgments that disturb him.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 19.8",["Mind & Self"]],["You are master of my carcase. Take it.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 19.10",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["For what purpose, then, have we received reason from nature? To make proper use of impressions.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 20.5",["Mind & Self"]],["Therefore the first and greatest task of a philosopher is to put impressions to the test and distinguish between them, and not admit any that has not been tested.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 20.7",["Mind & Self"]],["Who are these people, by whom you wish to be admired? Are they not the very people whom you have been in the habit of describing as mad? What, then, do you want to be admired by madmen?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 21.4",["Mind & Self"]],["Where, then, shall we place the good? To what class of things shall we apply it? To that of things that are in our power.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 22.11",["Mind & Self"]],["Henceforth, when some difficulty befalls you, remember that god, like a wrestling master, has matched you with a rough young man.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.1",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["For what end? That you may become an Olympic victor, and that cannot be done without sweat.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.2",["Virtue & Courage"]],["No man, in my opinion, has a more advantageous difficulty on his hands than you have, if only you will but use it as an athlete uses the young man he is wrestling against.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.2",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["He [Diogenes] says that death is no evil, for it is not dishonourable; that defamation is an empty noise made by madmen.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.6",["Death","Virtue & Courage"]],["He [Diogenes] says that to be naked is better than any purple-bordered robe, and that to sleep upon the bare ground is the softest bed.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.7",["Wealth","Happiness"]],["If you will but remember what is your own, you will not claim what belongs to others.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.11",["Mind & Self","Wealth"]],["Take this poor body of mine, take it all. If I can throw my body to this man, can I still be afraid of him?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.13",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["Do not be more cowardly than children, but just as they say, when the game no longer pleases them, 'I will play no more.'","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 24.20",["Death","Virtue & Courage"]],["If these things are true, and we are not stupid or acting a part when we say that good or ill for man lies in choice, and that all else is nothing to us, why are we still troubled?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.1",["Mind & Self"]],["No one else has authority over the things that seriously concern us; and the things over which others have authority are of no concern to us. What is left for us to worry about?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.2",["Mind & Self"]],["Guard by every means what is your own: what belongs to others do not covet. Your good faith is your own; your sense of shame is your own.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.4",["Virtue & Courage","Mind & Self"]],["When you concern yourself with what is not your own, you lose what is your own.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.4",["Mind & Self"]],["For you must always remember and hold fast to this, that the door is open.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.18",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["Thus Demetrius said to Nero: You threaten me with death, but nature threatens you with it!","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.22",["Death","Virtue & Courage"]],["If I place value on my body, I have given myself up for a slave; if on my miserable property, I am a slave likewise; for I immediately show to my own detriment how I may be taken.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.23-24",["Mind & Self","Wealth"]],["In matters relating to life no one offers himself to be examined.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 26.17",["Life","Mind & Self"]],["Socrates used to say that an unexamined life is not worth living.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 26.18",["Life","Mind & Self"]],["What aid, then, is it possible to discover against habit? The contrary habit.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 27.4",["Mind & Self","Virtue & Courage"]],["When death appears an evil, we ought immediately to remember that evils may be avoided, but death is a necessity.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 27.7",["Death"]],["I cannot escape death; but can I not escape the dread of it? Must I die trembling and lamenting? For the origin of the distress is wishing for something that does not come about.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 27.9-10",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["These are the ways in which human beings are undone; this is the siege, this the razing of a man's city, when his right judgements are demolished, when they are destroyed.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 28.25",["Mind & Self"]],["We ought, nevertheless, to prepare ourselves for this also, to be capable of being self-sufficient and bearing our own company.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 3, Chapter 13.6",["Mind & Self","Friendship"]],["Who is there left for me to fear? The man with power over what? Of things in my own power? Of these, no one is the master. Of things not in my power? And what are those to me?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 29.8",["Fear & Suffering","Mind & Self"]],["If you want anything good, get it from yourself.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 29.4",["Mind & Self","Happiness"]],["Nothing else can overcome the power of choice but that itself.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 29.12",["Mind & Self"]],["But he bought a lamp at the price of being a thief, a rogue, and a brute. That seemed to him a good bargain.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 29.21",["Wealth","Virtue & Courage"]],["I have learned to see that whatever happens, if it be outside the sphere of choice, is nothing to me.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 29.24",["Mind & Self"]],["When you are unable to change a person's view, recognize that he is a child, and clap your hands with him; or if you do not wish to do that, merely keep your silence.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 29.32",["Mind & Self"]],["When you appear before some man of authority, remember that there is another who looks down from above on what passes here, and that it is him whom you must please rather than this man.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 30.1",["Virtue & Courage","Fear & Suffering"]],["Freedom is not secured by the fulfilment of people's desires, but by the suppression of desire.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 4, Chapter 1.75",["Mind & Self","Happiness"]],["What is the first business of one who pursues philosophy? To cast away self-conceit. For it is impossible for any one to begin to learn what he thinks he knows.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 17.1",["Mind & Self"]],["Practice, for heaven's sake, in little things; and thence proceed to greater.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 18.18",["Virtue & Courage"]],["If you want to make progress, put up with being thought foolish and stupid with regards to externals.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 13",["Virtue & Courage","Mind & Self"]],["No man is free who is not master of himself.","Epictetus","Fragments, 35",["Mind & Self","Virtue & Courage"]],["You can be invincible if you enter into no contest in which it is not in your power to conquer.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 19",["Mind & Self","Virtue & Courage"]],["You should, then, accept the material you are given and set to work on it.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 5.22",["Life","Virtue & Courage"]],["I must die: and must I die groaning too? Be fettered. Must it be lamenting too? - Exiled. Can anyone prevent me, then, from going with a smile and good cheer and serenity?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 1.28",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["How much longer will you wait before you think yourself worthy of the best things and transgress in nothing the distinctions laid down by reason?","Epictetus","The Handbook, 51",["Virtue & Courage","Time"]],["If, instead of a man, a gentle and social creature, you have become a wild beast, dangerous, treacherous and liable to bite, have you lost nothing?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 10.14",["Virtue & Courage","Friendship"]],["Since he has harmed himself by wronging me, shall not I harm myself by wronging him?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 10.26",["Anger","Virtue & Courage"]],["Impression, wait for me a little. Let me see what you are and what you represent.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 18.24",["Mind & Self"]],["He was carried off to prison. The remark 'He has fared ill', is an addition that each man must make for himself.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 3, Chapter 8.5",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["What is a man? A part of a city, first, of that made up by gods which is a miniature of the universal city.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 5.26",["Life","Friendship"]],["Death, for instance, is nothing terrible, or else it would have appeared so to Socrates too. But the terror lies in our own judgment about death, that death is terrible.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 1.23",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["If you kiss your child, or your wife, say to yourself that it is a human being that you are kissing; and then you will not be disturbed if either of them dies.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 3",["Grief","Death"]],["Day by day you must keep before your eyes death and exile.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 21",["Death","Fear & Suffering"]],["For I am not eternal, but a man; a part of the whole, as an hour is of the day. Like an hour, I must come and, like an hour, must pass away.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 2, Chapter 5.13",["Death","Time"]],["If it ever happens that you turn to external things in the desire to please some other person, realize that you have ruined your scheme of life.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 23",["Mind & Self"]],["You will fetter my leg; but not even Zeus himself can get the better of my choice.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 1.23",["Mind & Self","Virtue & Courage"]],["When you relax your attention for a while, do not fancy you will recover it whenever you please.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 4, Chapter 12.1",["Mind & Self","Time"]],["Lay down from this moment a certain character and pattern of behaviour for yourself, which you will preserve both alone and in company.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 33",["Virtue & Courage"]],["But if, trembling and lamenting all the while, you seek never to fall into misfortune, how, I ask you, are you making progress?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 4.12",["Fear & Suffering","Virtue & Courage"]],["One man is not rendered unfortunate by another.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 9.34",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["For men do not marry and beget children in order to be miserable, but rather to be happy.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 11.3",["Happiness","Friendship"]],["Accept all things with contentment. What, then, is the punishment of those who do not? To be just as they are.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.21",["Happiness","Mind & Self"]],["What, then, must my leg be lame? And is it for this paltry leg, slave, that you accuse the universe?","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 12.24",["Fear & Suffering","Mind & Self"]],["No great thing comes into being all of a sudden.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 15.7",["Time","Virtue & Courage"]],["Put off my last tunic, this paltry body of mine: beyond this no one has any power over me.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 25.21",["Mind & Self","Fear & Suffering"]],["You hear ignorant people say, 'Poor soul, he is dead, his father died, his mother died, he was cut off before his time and in a foreign land.'","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 27.5",["Grief","Death"]],["If you are acting in harmony with nature, give me evidence of that, and I will say that you are making progress.","Epictetus","The Discourses, Book 1, Chapter 4.14",["Virtue & Courage","Life"]],["Never call yourself a philosopher, nor talk a great deal amongst laymen about philosophical principles, but do what follows from those principles.","Epictetus","The Handbook, 46",["Virtue & Courage"]]];
